Theory
Networking commands are built-in command-line utilities provided by operating systems such as
Windows and Linux. They are used to configure, monitor, test, and troubleshoot computer
networks. These commands help network administrators, engineers, and users identify
connectivity issues, verify network configurations, monitor active connections, and diagnose
communication problems between devices.
A computer communicates over a network using the TCP/IP protocol suite. Each device is
assigned a unique IP address and communicates through network devices such as switches,
routers, and modems. Networking commands allow users to inspect these configurations and
ensure that the network is functioning correctly.
These commands are commonly used in:
● Network installation and configuration
● Troubleshooting Internet connectivity
● Verifying IP address settings
● Testing communication between devices
● Monitoring network performance
● Diagnosing routing and DNS issues
● Checking active network connections
Networking Commands
11. ipconfig
Purpose-Displays the IP configuration of the computer.
2. ipconfig /all
Purpose-Displays detailed network configuration.
3. ping
Purpose-Tests connectivity between two computers.
4. tracert
Purpose-Shows the route taken by packets from source to destination.
5. hostname
Purpose-Displays the computer name.
6. getmac
Purpose-Displays the MAC (Physical) Address
7. arp -a
Purpose-Displays the ARP (Address Resolution Protocol) cache.
8. netstat
Purpose-Displays active network connections.
9. nslookup
Purpose-Finds the IP address of a domain name.
10. pathping
Combines the features of ping and tracert to identify packet loss and latency.
Syntax
pathping google.com
